{"name":"Soft Coral","hex":"#ffeee0","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soft coral","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the order Alcyonacea of corals that do not produce calcium carbonate skeletons."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soft_coral"}],"entries":[{"title":"Alcyonacea","blocks":[{"text":"Alcyonacea, in English soft corals or alcyonacians, is a former order of Octocorallia. Since a 2022 revision of Octocorallia, the content of Alcyonacea has been included in the order Malacalcyonacea and, to a lesser extent, in the order Scleralcyonacea, these two new orders now making up the order Octocorallia."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ef/Cladiella.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcyonacea"},{"title":"Octocorallia","blocks":[{"text":"Octocorallia, along with Hexacorallia, is one of the two extant classes of Anthozoa. It comprises over 3,000 species of marine and brackish animals consisting of colonial polyps with eight-fold symmetry, commonly referred to informally as \"soft corals\". It was previously known by the now-unaccepted scientific names Alcyonacea and Gorgonacea, both deprecated c. 2022, and by the also deprecated name of Alcyonaria, in earlier times."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Pink_soft_coral_Nick_Hobgood.jpg/330px-Pink_soft_coral_Nick_Hobgood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Octocorallia"},{"title":"Coral","blocks":[{"text":"Corals are colonial marine invertebrates within the subphylum Anthozoa of the phylum Cnidaria. They typically form compact colonies of many identical individual polyps. Coral species include the important reef builders that inhabit tropical oceans and secrete calcium carbonate to form a hard skeleton."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg/330px-Coral_Outcrop_Flynn_Reef.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral"},{"title":"Coral (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The various tones of the color coral are orange, red and pink representations of the colors of those cnidarians known as precious corals."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Venusin_vejar.jpg/250px-Venusin_vejar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Venusin_vejar.jpg/500px-Venusin_vejar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Venusin_vejar.jpg/250px-Venusin_vejar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVenusin_vejar.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Gorgoniidae-Reproduction.jpg/250px-Gorgoniidae-Reproduction.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Gorgoniidae-Reproduction.jpg/500px-Gorgoniidae-Reproduction.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Gorgoniidae-Reproduction.jpg/250px-Gorgoniidae-Reproduction.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGorgoniidae-Reproduction.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/1898_aquaimages.jpg/250px-1898_aquaimages.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/1898_aquaim